MVM.registerModule({
  "id": "m15",
  "summary": "Service and diagnose vehicle air conditioning, heating and ventilation systems safely and legally, using gauges, recovery equipment and a logical fault-finding method.",
  "outcomes": ["Explain who may service an A/C system, why refrigerant must never be vented, and the hazards involved.","Connect a manifold gauge set and interpret pressure readings at different temperatures and engine speeds.","Describe how to recover refrigerant, evacuate, check for leaks, add oil and recharge by weight.","Diagnose no cooling, weak cooling, noise, icing and clutch faults with a logical flow of checks.","Describe the heater core, blower, blend doors and air distribution, and how air is directed for heating and demisting.","Diagnose actuator, sensor, blower and heater core faults and advise customers on comfort in hot climates."],
  "cheatsheet": ["In many countries only a certified technician may open or recharge an A/C system. Never vent refrigerant.","Gauge hoses: blue is low side, red is high side, yellow is for service. Always wear eye and skin protection.","Static pressure (engine off) is equal on both sides and rises with temperature.","Low on both gauges usually means low refrigerant. High on both usually means overcharge or poor condenser cooling.","Recover, evacuate, check for leaks, then charge by weight with the correct oil.","Leak detection: UV dye, electronic detector or soapy water. A system that will not hold vacuum has a leak.","Heater: coolant flows through the heater core and the blower pushes air across it.","Blend doors mix hot and cold air. Actuators move the doors and can be tested by command with a scan tool.","To demist, use fresh air, A/C on to dry the air and heat to warm it."],
  "lessons": [
    {"id": "m15-l1", "title": "Service safety and the law", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Servicing an air conditioning system involves hazardous chemicals under pressure and, in most countries, legal rules. Know these rules before you ever touch a gauge."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Who may work on A/C systems"},
      {"t":"p","text":"In many countries, only a **certified technician** with the right training may service an A/C system, buy refrigerant or open the refrigerant circuit. The rules differ between countries, so check your local law. Employers must provide approved equipment. Even where the law is relaxed, the dangers remain, and untrained work often leads to a wrong charge, a damaged compressor and a polluted atmosphere."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Never vent refrigerant"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Refrigerant must never be released to the air. It must be **recovered** into a certified recovery machine, then recycled or sent for safe disposal. Do not disconnect hoses to see 'if it hisses'. Keep records of refrigerant recovered and added, and the equipment used, as required locally."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Hazards"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Frostbite and eye damage:** liquid refrigerant boils at a very low temperature and freezes skin and eyes on contact. Wear safety goggles or a face shield and gloves.","**High pressure:** an A/C system can be over 15 bar (220 psi) on the high side. Never heat a cylinder or a part of the system with a flame.","**Suffocation:** refrigerant gas is heavier than air and can collect in a pit or a low area. Work with good ventilation.","**Fire and toxic gas:** mildly flammable refrigerant, such as R1234yf, can ignite near a hot surface or a flame. A burning refrigerant produces toxic gases.","**Moving parts:** keep clear of belts, the clutch and fans."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Preparing to work"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Identify the refrigerant and the oil from the label.","Use only equipment designed for that refrigerant. A machine for R134a cannot be used for R1234yf.","Wear goggles and gloves, and keep ignition sources away.","Check the system for obvious damage and leaks before you connect gauges."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Looking after the equipment"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Have the recovery and charging machine **calibrated and serviced** at the intervals the maker or the law requires. A scale that is wrong by 50 g gives a wrong charge.","Change the machine's **filters and drier** when they are due, and drain the oil separator and record the quantity recovered, so it can be added back.","Keep hoses and couplers in good order. A leaking coupler loses refrigerant and lets in air and moisture.","Store refrigerant cylinders upright and away from heat, secured, with their valves closed and capped.","Use a separate machine, or a machine designed for it, for each refrigerant. A machine that has handled R134a must not be used on R1234yf without the right conversion.","Keep gauge sets and hoses clean and marked for the refrigerant they were used with."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Never put a cylinder of refrigerant in a hot place, and never fill a cylinder beyond its limit. Never use compressed air or oxygen to pressure test an A/C system, because it can explode with the oil in the system."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"If you are not certified or equipped, do not open the system. Refer the vehicle to someone who is."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m15-l2", "title": "Gauges and readings", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"The manifold gauge set tells you the pressure on each side of the A/C system. With a thermometer, it is the main tool for diagnosis. This lesson explains how to read it."},
      {"t":"diagram","name":"ac-gauge-readings"},
      {"t":"h","text":"The manifold gauge set"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A manifold gauge set has two gauges and three hoses. The **blue** gauge and hose connect to the low-side service port. The **red** gauge and hose connect to the high-side service port. The **yellow** hose in the centre goes to the recovery machine, the vacuum pump or the refrigerant supply. Quick-connect couplers fit only the correct port, since the high and low ports have different sizes. The gauge set must be suitable for the refrigerant. Never mix the equipment between refrigerants."},
      {"t":"diagram","name":"ac-manifold-hookup"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Static pressure"},
      {"t":"p","text":"With the engine off and the system rested, the pressure is the same on both sides and depends on the temperature. For R134a at around 25 °C, it is typically about 6 to 7 bar (about 85 to 100 psi), and it rises as the temperature rises. A static pressure much lower than this suggests a low charge, because there is not enough refrigerant to match the temperature. A system with no pressure has leaked out completely and has to be leak tested and evacuated."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Operating pressures"},
      {"t":"p","text":"With the engine at about 1,500 rpm, the A/C on, the blower on high and the doors open, typical pressures at an ambient temperature of around 30 °C are about 1.5 to 2.5 bar (22 to 36 psi) on the low side, and 12 to 14 bar (175 to 205 psi) on the high side for R134a. Pressures rise with ambient temperature and humidity. Use the chart in the manual."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Readings","Likely cause"],"rows":[["Low side low, high side low","Low refrigerant charge (leak)"],["Low side high, high side high","Overcharge, or poor cooling of the condenser (blocked, dirty or fan not working), or air in the system"],["Low side high, high side low","Weak compressor, or the compressor is not pumping properly"],["Low side very low or vacuum, high side high","Restriction, such as a blocked orifice tube or expansion valve"],["Low side cycling between vacuum and normal","Moisture freezing at the expansion device"]],"caption":"Typical gauge readings and causes"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Vent temperature and humidity"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Measure the temperature at the centre vent, with the system running at about 1,500 rpm, the blower on high and recirculation on. A healthy system gives a vent temperature of roughly 5 to 10 °C in mild weather. A high humidity, a hot day or a high cabin temperature raises the reading."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Connecting the gauges safely"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Check that the engine is off and the system valves on the manifold are closed.","Remove the dust caps from the service ports. Check for a refrigerant type label.","Connect the blue hose to the low-side port, and the red hose to the high-side port, with the coupler valves open on the manifold ends only after the couplers are fitted. Use the right size of coupler for the refrigerant.","Purge the hoses of air if the procedure calls for it, by briefly cracking the coupler at the manifold with refrigerant behind it, or use the machine's function.","Take the readings, and then close the valves before you remove the couplers. Cap the ports."]},
      {"t":"p","text":"Keep the high side coupler away from your face and body when you disconnect it. A small amount of refrigerant can escape and freeze skin. Use gloves and eye protection every time."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Gauge readings point to a group of possible faults. They do not name one. Combine them with vent temperature, visual checks and electrical tests."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"Always compare your readings with the ambient temperature and the chart. A pressure that is normal at 20 °C is low at 40 °C."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m15-l3", "title": "Finding and repairing refrigerant leaks", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Almost every air-conditioning system loses a little refrigerant over time. A system that loses it quickly, or that has lost it all, has a leak that must be found and repaired. Recharging without finding the leak wastes the customer's money and pollutes the air. This lesson explains the methods for finding leaks and the right way to repair them."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Where leaks happen"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Place","Why it leaks"],"rows":[["Compressor shaft seal","Wear. Oil around the front of the compressor and clutch."],["Hose crimps and fittings","Vibration and age. Oil stain at the crimp or the connection."],["O-rings at joints","Hardening, damage, wrong type. Oil at the joint."],["Condenser","Stone damage and corrosion at the front of the car"],["Evaporator","Corrosion inside the dashboard box. Hard to see, shown by dye in the drain or a detector at the vent."],["Service ports and valves","Faulty valve cores. Dust caps missing."],["Rubber hoses","Refrigerant slowly passes through the rubber (permeation), which is normal at a very low rate."]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Methods of leak detection"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Method","How it works","Good for","Limits"],"rows":[["Visual and oil traces","Refrigerant carries oil out. Look for an oily, dusty patch.","Joints, hoses, compressor","Hidden places, slow leaks"],["Electronic detector","A sensor 'sniffs' refrigerant and beeps. Move slowly, at about 25 mm per second, below the line, as refrigerant is heavier than air.","Finding small leaks at joints","Draughts and other chemicals give false readings"],["UV dye","A fluorescent dye is added to the system and found with a UV lamp and yellow glasses after running","Slow leaks, and leaks in hidden places","Needs time to show, and a clean system"],["Bubble solution","Foam on the fitting forms bubbles at a leak","Accessible joints","Cannot find leaks in hidden places"],["Nitrogen pressure test","The empty system is pressurised with nitrogen through a regulator and watched on a gauge. A drop shows a leak, and a hiss or bubble solution locates it.","Large leaks, and a pressure hold test after a repair","Needs the correct regulator and care"],["Vacuum hold test","After evacuating, the system is isolated and the vacuum is watched","Showing a leak after repair","Moisture boiling off can cause a false rise"]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"A nitrogen pressure test"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Recover the refrigerant fully. Never pressurise a system that still holds refrigerant with nitrogen.","Connect a nitrogen cylinder with a **pressure regulator** and a relief valve to the service port. Never connect a cylinder directly to the system.","Raise the pressure slowly to the value in the manual, typically no more than about 10 bar (145 psi). Do not exceed it.","Close the valve and watch the gauge. A fall shows a leak. Use bubble solution on joints, and listen.","Release the nitrogen safely to the open air through the gauge manifold, and repair the leak.","Evacuate before recharging, as the nitrogen is dry but may have carried air."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Repairing a leak"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**O-rings:** replace with the correct type, size and material for the refrigerant. Lubricate with the right oil, not grease or petroleum jelly.","**Hoses:** replace the whole hose assembly, or have the crimp renewed by a specialist. Never clamp over a leaking crimp.","**Condenser and evaporator:** replace. These cannot be reliably repaired. Fit the right amount of oil.","**Compressor shaft seal:** replace the seal if it can be replaced, or the whole compressor if the seal is not serviceable.","**Service port cores:** replace the core with a tool, after recovering the refrigerant.","After every repair, replace the receiver-drier or accumulator if the system was open for more than a short time."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Stop-leak products"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Many cans of 'stop leak' or sealant are sold for air conditioning. They are not a repair. They can block the expansion valve, the orifice tube and the drier, and can contaminate the service equipment with sticky material that is expensive to clear. Many workshops will refuse to service a system that contains a sealant. Do not use them, and warn customers who ask."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Find the leak before you recharge. A recharge without a repair is a temporary and unlawful top-up."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Never pressurise an A/C system with compressed air, and never apply more than the specified nitrogen pressure. A system that bursts can injure you."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m15-l4", "title": "Recover, evacuate and recharge", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"When an A/C system has to be opened or has lost its charge, the correct sequence is recover, repair, evacuate, check and recharge. This lesson explains each step."},
      {"t":"diagram","name":"ac-service-sequence"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Recover and repair"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Connect the recovery machine to the service ports, and recover the refrigerant. The machine measures the amount that came out, which tells you how much oil came with it, and whether the system had its full charge. Never discharge the refrigerant to the air."},
      {"t":"p","text":"With the system empty, replace the faulty part. Cap all openings straight away to keep moisture and dirt out. Fit new O-rings of the correct material and lubricate them with the correct oil. Replace the drier or accumulator whenever the system has been opened for a major repair. Add the quantity of oil that the new part needs, as the manual says."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Evacuate and check for leaks"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Connect a **vacuum pump** and pull the system down into a deep vacuum for at least 20 to 30 minutes, or as the manual says. This boils off moisture and removes air. Then close the valves and watch the vacuum gauge for about 10 minutes. If the vacuum falls, there is a leak, which has to be found and repaired. A system that holds vacuum has no large leak."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Charge by weight"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Add the exact weight of refrigerant shown on the label, using a charging scale or a charging station. Do not charge by pressure alone, because the pressure depends on the temperature. Too little refrigerant gives poor cooling and poor lubrication, and too much causes high pressure and poor cooling. Check the oil quantity as well."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Leak detection and contamination"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**UV dye:** add a measured amount of dye to the system and look for glowing marks with a UV lamp after running it.","**Electronic leak detector:** a sensor that beeps when it finds refrigerant. Move slowly along the pipes, fittings and parts, especially at joints and the lowest points.","**Soapy water:** bubbles form at a leak on an accessible joint."]},
      {"t":"p","text":"If a compressor fails, metal debris can spread through the system. In that case, flush the condenser, evaporator and lines with an approved flushing agent, if the maker allows it, and replace the compressor, the drier or accumulator, and the orifice tube or expansion valve. Many modern condensers (parallel flow types) can not be flushed and must be replaced."},
      {"t":"example","title":"Working out the charge","steps":["The label under the bonnet shows a charge of 550 g. The recovery machine took out 480 g from the system, so the system was 70 g low. There may have been a leak.","After repairs and evacuation, the machine is set to charge 550 g, the weight on the label. Do not charge the 480 g recovered.","Some machines add the oil and the dye as well. If the system lost oil, add the quantity the machine recovered (the machine's oil bottle shows it), plus extra for any part replaced.","After the charge, run the system and check the readings are in the normal range for the ambient temperature."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Never use a vacuum pump without checking that all the valves are in the right position, and never pull a vacuum on a system that contains refrigerant."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Weight, not pressure, is the way to charge a system. Use the scale."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m15-l5", "title": "Using recovery stations, vacuum pumps and charging scales", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"The equipment in an air-conditioning bay is specialised. The recovery and recycling machine, vacuum pump and scales are designed for refrigerant, and each has its own rules. This lesson explains how to use them correctly, what a good vacuum looks like and how to charge accurately."},
      {"t":"h","text":"The recovery machine"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A recovery and recycling station draws refrigerant out of the vehicle, filters it, separates the oil and stores the clean refrigerant in an internal cylinder, weighed on a scale. Many are **automatic**, with a sequence of recover, evacuate and recharge. Each step needs the right setting and attention."},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Connect the machine's hoses to the vehicle ports and open the valves.","Start the **recovery**. The machine pulls the refrigerant out until the pressure falls to a low value, then pauses for a few minutes to let the remaining refrigerant evaporate from the oil, and takes more. A system may need this cycle repeated until the pressure stays under a set level.","Read the **weight recovered** on the machine's display, and record it. Note the quantity of oil that was separated.","Run the **vacuum** stage (see below).","Add oil, dye if wanted, and then the refrigerant by weight, as set on the screen.","Remove the hoses, with the machine's hoses sealed to prevent loss and contamination."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"How a vacuum works"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The aim of evacuation is to remove air and moisture. Air in the system raises the high-side pressure and wastes capacity. Moisture forms acid and ice. A vacuum pump lowers the pressure inside the system until water boils at the temperature of the system. At sea level, water boils at 100 °C at normal pressure but only at about 25 °C when the pressure has dropped to about 3 kPa absolute. Moisture then evaporates and is carried away by the pump. This is why a deep vacuum is needed, and why a quick pump-down for a few minutes is not enough."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Level of vacuum","Absolute pressure","What it means"],"rows":[["Normal atmosphere","About 101 kPa","No vacuum"],["Good vacuum","About 10 kPa or less","Air removed. Moisture still present."],["Water boils at about 25 °C","About 3 kPa","Moisture starts to evaporate at room temperature"],["Deep vacuum","About 0.1 kPa (about 750 microns) or less","Moisture removed. The standard to aim for, as the manual states."]],"caption":"A gauge that reads in kPa below atmospheric shows about −100 kPa (or 29.9 inHg) at a deep vacuum at sea level. Use a vacuum gauge (micron gauge) for accuracy, as a manifold gauge cannot show a deep vacuum precisely."},
      {"t":"h","text":"The hold test"},
      {"t":"p","text":"After evacuating for the time stated, usually 20 to 30 minutes or more, close the valves and switch off the pump. Watch the vacuum for 10 to 15 minutes. It should hold with a rise of no more than a small amount, about 1 to 3 kPa. A large rise shows a leak, or a lot of moisture still boiling out. Find and repair the leak, or evacuate longer. Hold tests are a quick way to see a leak before charging."},
      {"t":"example","title":"Reading a vacuum hold test","steps":["The pump reaches 0.2 kPa absolute (about 1,500 microns) after 30 minutes. The valves are closed and the pump off.","After 15 minutes the pressure has risen to 0.5 kPa and is steady. This is a small rise that levels off. It may be moisture still in the system, and the system can be evacuated again for longer.","If it had climbed steadily to 10 kPa and kept rising, there would be a leak. Pressurise with nitrogen and find it."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Charging by weight"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Use the machine's electronic scale, or a separate calibrated scale, that reads to within a few grams.","Charge **liquid** refrigerant into the high side only with the engine off, or **vapour** into the low side with the engine running, as the method and the machine allow. Never charge liquid into the low side of a running system.","After the weight has been added, start the engine and the A/C and check the pressures and the vent temperature. Do not add more to improve the readings, unless there is an error in the weight.","Record the amount charged on the job card, and on a label if the law requires."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Why accuracy matters"},
      {"t":"example","title":"The cost of a wrong charge","steps":["A system needs 550 g. A charge of 650 g, which is 100 g over, raises the high-side pressure by 2 to 4 bar. The compressor works harder, the condenser cannot reject the heat and cooling falls.","A charge of 450 g, 100 g under, leaves the evaporator starved. The superheat rises, the cooling is weak and the compressor may run without enough oil, as the oil circulates with the refrigerant.","Both mistakes are only about 18% off, but they cause measurable problems. This is why weight, not feel or a gauge, sets the charge."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"The machine can only be as accurate as its calibration and the hoses are tight. Check them regularly."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"Keep the vacuum pump oil clean. Change it at the intervals stated, because moisture-laden oil cannot give a deep vacuum."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m15-l6", "title": "A/C faults and diagnosis", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Most A/C complaints can be solved with a logical series of checks. This lesson gives you that sequence."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Questions and visual checks"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Ask the customer what the problem is: no cooling, weak cooling, or intermittent. When does it happen? Check the compressor belt, the condenser for blockage, the cabin filter, the condenser fan and the clutch. Look for oil stains on pipes and fittings, which show a leak."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Symptoms and causes"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Symptom","Likely causes"],"rows":[["No cooling at all","No refrigerant, compressor not running (fuse, relay, switch, clutch), seized compressor, failed blend door"],["Weak cooling","Low charge, blocked condenser, weak fan, blocked cabin filter, partly blocked expansion device, air in the system"],["Cools then stops (intermittent)","Moisture freezing, a faulty sensor or switch, a clutch with a bad air gap, a compressor cycling too quickly"],["Noise","Worn compressor, loose clutch, low oil, noisy belt or bearing, a rattling expansion valve"],["Clutch will not engage","No power, blown fuse, open coil, low-pressure switch open, bad ground, wrong air gap"],["Icing at the vents","A frozen evaporator, from weak airflow or a faulty temperature sensor"],["Bad smell","Mould in the evaporator or a blocked drain"]],"caption":"A/C faults"},
      {"t":"h","text":"A diagnostic flow"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Check the vehicle: belt, fan, condenser, filter, drain and the label.","Switch on the A/C, and check that the clutch engages and the fans run.","Measure the vent temperature.","Connect the gauges and read the static and operating pressures.","Use the readings to narrow down the fault, and test the likely causes.","Repair, evacuate and recharge as needed, and test again."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Electrical checks"},
      {"t":"p","text":"If the clutch does not engage, check the fuse, then the relay, then the pressure switches and the temperature sensor with a multimeter and the wiring diagram. Check for power at the clutch coil with the A/C switched on. Measure the coil resistance, which is typically a few ohms (check the manual). Use a scan tool to see whether the engine computer is allowing the A/C to run."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Sight glass and feel"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Some systems have a sight glass in the receiver-drier. A steady stream of bubbles when the system is running suggests a low charge, and a clear glass means liquid only (full or empty, check the pressures). Foam that appears and disappears may be normal when the compressor starts. Do not use the sight glass alone to decide the charge, because systems vary. Feel the pipes carefully: the high-pressure line should be warm to hot and the low-pressure line cold and sweating. A single place where the temperature changes sharply on a pipe, such as at a drier or valve, shows a restriction."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Questions to ask the customer"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["When did it start? Suddenly, or gradually over weeks? A sudden loss of cooling points to a failed part, and a gradual one points to a leak.","Does it cool at some speeds and not others? Cool on the move but warm at idle suggests the condenser fan or airflow.","Does it cool well in the morning and poorly in the afternoon? A freezing evaporator or a weak charge may show only in some conditions.","Is there a smell, a noise, or water inside the car? These point to the drain, the heater core, the blower or the compressor.","Has the system been recharged before, and how recently? Frequent recharges mean a leak.","Has any other work been done, such as a collision repair, a radiator replacement or an engine change?"]},
      {"t":"p","text":"Write the answers on the job card. Together with the gauge readings, they give a more complete picture than either does alone, and they show the customer that you have listened."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"If the system is low on refrigerant, do not just top up. Find the leak. A system that has lost refrigerant has leaked, and it will leak again."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Never jump the clutch directly to the battery on a system with a low charge or unknown pressure. The compressor can be damaged very quickly without oil."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m15-l7", "title": "Gauge diagnosis in depth: patterns and case studies", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"A manifold gauge set gives two numbers. By themselves, they do not name the fault. Together with the ambient temperature, the vent temperature and what you can see and feel, they point to a small group of causes. This lesson gives a systematic way to interpret readings and shows how to use them in real diagnosis."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Before you read the gauges"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Set the conditions: engine at about 1,500 rpm, doors and windows open, A/C on maximum cold and the blower at high, recirculation on, and a fan in front of the car if the ambient temperature is high.","Let the system stabilise for several minutes.","Note the **ambient temperature** and the **humidity**. High-side pressure rises with ambient temperature.","Measure the **centre vent temperature** with a thermometer."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Normal readings at different outside temperatures (R134a, about 1,500 rpm)"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Ambient temperature","Low side (bar)","High side (bar)","Centre vent temperature"],"rows":[["21 °C","About 1.5 to 2.0","About 10 to 12","About 2 to 5 °C"],["27 °C","About 1.5 to 2.2","About 12 to 14","About 4 to 8 °C"],["32 °C","About 1.8 to 2.5","About 14 to 16","About 7 to 11 °C"],["38 °C","About 2.0 to 3.0","About 16 to 20","About 11 to 16 °C"],["43 °C","About 2.5 to 3.5","About 19 to 23","About 15 to 20 °C"]],"caption":"Use these as a guide. Many makers publish a chart for their own system. Higher humidity raises the vent temperature."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Fault patterns"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Low side","High side","Other clues","Likely cause"],"rows":[["Low","Low","Weak cooling, bubbles at sight glass, compressor cycles quickly","Low charge from a leak"],["High","High","Hot hoses, condenser very hot, poor cooling","Overcharge, or too little air over the condenser, or air in the system"],["High","Low","The compressor runs but pressures are close together, little cold","Weak compressor, worn valves or a leaking seal"],["Very low or vacuum","High or normal","Frost or a cold spot at a pipe, poor cooling","A restriction: a blocked orifice tube, expansion valve or drier"],["Cycles between vacuum and normal","Normal","Cooling falls off and returns","Moisture freezing at the expansion device"],["Normal to high","Very high, needle unsteady","Poor cooling, high compressor load","Air or non-condensable gas in the system"],["Low","Normal","Frost on the evaporator or a cold suction line","Airflow problem at the evaporator: a dirty cabin filter or blower fault"]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Case 1: Weak cooling on a hot day"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A car at 35 °C ambient gives vent air at 18 °C. Gauges read 1.0 bar low and 8 bar high. Both are low for the temperature. The static pressure at 35 °C was 3 bar, well under the 7 or 8 bar expected, so the system has lost much of its charge. The technician finds oil at the compressor hose crimp using a UV lamp. A new hose, a new drier and an evacuation, then a charge by weight, bring the readings to 2.3 bar and 17 bar with a vent temperature of 9 °C."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Case 2: Cool at speed, warm at idle"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A van cools well on the road but the vent air warms at idle. At idle the gauges read 3.4 bar low and 26 bar high. The high side is very high. The condenser fan is not running. The fan relay is found with burnt contacts. The new relay restores the fan, and at idle the pressures fall to 2.4 and 17 bar."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Case 3: No cooling and a frosted pipe"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A system cools weakly and the pipe leaving the receiver-drier is frosted. Gauges read 0.2 bar low and 14 bar high. The low side is very low and the high side is high, which points to a restriction. The inlet and outlet of the drier differ by 25 °C. The drier's screen is blocked with debris from an old compressor failure. The compressor, drier and valve are replaced, with a system flush, and the readings return to normal."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Putting it together"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Always compare gauge readings with the ambient temperature.","Use a second source of information, such as pipe temperatures, vent temperature and a visual check.","Confirm the fault with a test or a measurement before you replace a part. For instance, check the fan operation, the airflow or the compressor clutch.","After a repair, recheck the pressures and the vent temperature to prove the fix.","Write the readings and conditions on the job card for the customer and for your own reference."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Gauge readings point to a group of faults. Use them with temperature, sight, touch and a second test to find the one."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"If readings look strange, check your equipment first. A blocked hose, a faulty gauge or a wrongly set machine can mimic a fault."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m15-l8", "title": "Heating and ventilation", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"The same air box that cools the cabin also heats and ventilates it. This lesson explains how heating and air distribution work."},
      {"t":"diagram","name":"hvac-air-distribution"},
      {"t":"h","text":"The heater core"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The **heater core** is a small radiator in the HVAC box. Hot coolant from the engine flows through it, and the blower pushes cabin air across it, so the air warms up. A coolant valve on some vehicles or a blend door controls how much heat is delivered. If the heater is cold, check the coolant level, the thermostat, air in the cooling system and whether the heater core is blocked."},
      {"t":"h","text":"The blower"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A **blower motor** with a fan wheel pushes air through the box. The speed is controlled by a **resistor pack** with a number of steps, or by an electronic module with PWM. A common fault is that the blower works only on high speed, because the resistor pack has burned out. A blower that is noisy or weak may have a worn motor, a blocked cabin filter or debris in the fan."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Air distribution and doors"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Doors, or flaps, inside the box direct the air. The **mode door** sends air to the face vents, the feet or the windscreen. The **blend door** controls how much of the air passes over the heater core, and so controls the temperature. The **recirculation door** chooses between recirculating cabin air, which cools faster, and fresh air from outside. On automatic systems, small actuator motors move the doors."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Demisting"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Fog on the inside of the windscreen is moisture in the cabin air condensing on the cold glass. To clear it, use fresh air (not recirculation), switch on the A/C to dry the air, turn on the heat and direct the air to the windscreen. The A/C dehumidifies the air, which is why many systems turn the compressor on when you select demist."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Cabin filter"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The cabin filter cleans the air entering the box. A blocked one reduces airflow, makes the blower noisier and can cause weak cooling and heating and misting. Replace it at the maker's interval, or more often in dusty areas."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Control","What it does"],"rows":[["Blend door","Sets the temperature by mixing hot and cold air"],["Mode door","Chooses face, feet and windscreen outlets"],["Recirculation door","Chooses cabin air or fresh outside air"],["Blower speed","Sets the airflow"]],"caption":"HVAC controls"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Testing the heater"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Test","How","Result"],"rows":[["Hose temperatures","Feel or measure the two heater hoses at the bulkhead with the engine hot","Both should be about the same, at near engine temperature. One hot, one cold, means the core is blocked."],["Coolant flow","Check the water pump, the thermostat and the heater control valve","Flow through the core with the heater on"],["Heater core pressure","Pressure test the system and look for a drop with no external leak","A drop with damp carpet and misted windows suggests a leaking core"],["Air lock","Gurgling noise, cool air at idle but warm when revving","Bleed the system again, with the heater on hot"],["Blend door","Set the temperature to hot and cold and listen and look for door movement","A door that does not move gives the wrong temperature whatever the core is doing"]]},
      {"t":"p","text":"If a heater core has to be replaced, many cars require the dashboard to be removed, which is a long job. Make sure of the diagnosis first, since the heater matrix may only be blocked and can often be flushed, and tell the customer the cost and time before you start."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"In dusty regions, a cabin filter may need changing every few months. A clean filter makes the whole system work better."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Cooling and heating share the same airflow path. A blocked filter or a stuck door can cause faults in both."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m15-l9", "title": "Heating, ventilation and air quality", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"A heater and ventilation system is not only for comfort. It clears the windscreen and keeps the driver alert and healthy. Faults can leave a driver unable to see. This lesson explains how misting happens, how the heater and the cabin air work together, and how to deal with smells and poor air quality."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Why windows mist"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Air holds water vapour, and holds more when it is warm. If it touches a surface colder than its **dew point**, the vapour condenses into droplets. The dew point of cabin air depends on its humidity. Breathing, wet clothes and a damp carpet raise it."},
      {"t":"example","title":"When will a windscreen mist?","steps":["The cabin air is 22 °C and 70% relative humidity. Its dew point is about 16 °C.","On a cold morning the glass is at 8 °C, which is below 16 °C, so water condenses on the glass and it mists.","To clear it, the air must be dried, so that its dew point falls below the glass temperature, and the glass must be warmed. This is why the air conditioner is switched on for demisting: the cold evaporator removes moisture, at about 3 °C it can bring the dew point down to near 3 °C, and the heater then warms the dry air.","Using recirculation when the glass is misting makes things worse, because the moist air is kept inside. Use fresh air."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"The air path"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Intake:** air enters at the base of the windscreen, through a grille and a cabin filter. Leaves and dirt must be cleared from the intake and the drains.","**Blower:** pushes the air through the box.","**Evaporator:** cools and dries the air when the A/C is on.","**Heater core and blend door:** warms the air to the temperature set.","**Mode doors:** send air to the windscreen, face and feet.","**Outlets and extraction:** air leaves through vents in the body, usually near the boot or rear pillars."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"The cabin filter"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Filter","What it traps","Notes"],"rows":[["Particle (paper or fibre)","Dust, pollen, soot","Cheapest. Replace at the interval or more often in dust."],["Activated carbon","Particles plus odours and gases such as exhaust fumes","Better in traffic. Replace at the interval."],["Combination or anti-allergen","Finer particles, pollen and allergens","For allergy sufferers"]]},
      {"t":"p","text":"A blocked filter reduces airflow, leads to poor cooling and heating and misted windows, and can make the blower noisy. In dusty regions it may need replacing every few months. It is often behind the glovebox or under the bonnet. Fit it in the direction of the airflow arrow."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Smells and what they mean"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Smell","Likely cause","Action"],"rows":[["Musty or mouldy, worse when starting the A/C","Mould in a wet evaporator box or a blocked drain","Clear the drain, replace the filter, treat the evaporator box with an approved cleaner"],["Sweet, with misted windows and wet carpet","A leaking heater core, coolant escaping","Pressure test, repair or replace the heater core. The coolant is poisonous."],["Burning dust at first heater use of the season","Dust on the heating element or resistor","Normal for a short time"],["Burning plastic or hot electrical smell","An overheated blower motor, resistor or connector","Stop using it. Check the blower circuit."],["Exhaust or petrol","A leak in the exhaust or a fuel leak, or air from the engine bay drawn in","Find the source. Carbon monoxide is dangerous."],["Rotten egg or sulphur","Overheated or failing catalytic converter, or a battery overcharging","Test the system voltage and the exhaust"]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Moulds and bacteria"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The evaporator is cool and wet, and in the dark. Mould and bacteria grow there. Prevention is better than cure: switch off the A/C a minute before arriving, while the blower runs, to dry the evaporator, and keep the drain clear. If a smell is present, use an approved evaporator cleaner or a disinfectant treatment sprayed into the box or the intake as its instructions state, with the windows open, and replace the cabin filter."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Carbon monoxide"},
      {"t":"p","text":"An exhaust leak near the cabin can send carbon monoxide into the vehicle. CO has no smell, but it causes headache, drowsiness and confusion, and can kill. If a customer reports drowsiness or headaches when driving with the heater on, check the exhaust for leaks and the floor and bulkhead seals. Advise them to open the windows. Test the exhaust system when the vehicle is on a lift."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Fresh air, a clean filter, a clear drain and a dry evaporator keep a cabin healthy. Misting is a humidity problem, not just a heater problem."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Never ignore a smell of exhaust inside a vehicle. Carbon monoxide has no smell of its own, and a vehicle with an exhaust leak into the cabin must be repaired before it is driven."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m15-l10", "title": "Climate control faults", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Climate faults are often small mechanical or electrical problems in the air box. This lesson shows you how to find them, and how to talk to the customer about comfort."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Door and actuator faults"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A clicking or ticking sound from behind the dashboard when the temperature is changed often means that an actuator gear has stripped, or that a door has jammed. Symptoms include air that is hot or cold on one side only, air that comes out of the wrong vents, or a temperature that can not be changed. Use a scan tool to command the actuators and watch their feedback, or watch and feel the door move. After replacing an actuator, a **calibration** (relearn) with a scan tool is often needed."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Temperature sensors and blower faults"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Automatic systems rely on sensors for the cabin, the ambient air, the evaporator and the sun load. A failed sensor can cause the wrong temperature, the compressor not running or the blower running at the wrong speed. Compare the sensor reading on the scan tool with a thermometer."},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Works only on high: burned resistor pack.","No blower at all: fuse, relay, switch or motor.","Noise that changes with speed: debris in the fan or a worn motor.","Weak airflow: blocked cabin filter or a failed recirculation door."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Heater core leaks and steam"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A leaking heater core shows as a **wet carpet** on the driver or passenger side, a sweet coolant smell, a greasy film on the inside of the windscreen, and coolant loss with no external leak. Confirm with a pressure test of the cooling system. A heater core often needs the dashboard to be removed, so it is a long job. A bypass of the heater hoses is only a short-term emergency fix, and the customer must be told."},
      {"t":"p","text":"Steam on the windscreen with the heater on can mean a leaking heater core. Moisture inside the car on a rainy day can also be caused by a blocked drain or a wet carpet. Find the source."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Customer advice for hot climates"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["On a very hot day, open the windows and doors for a minute to let the hot air out before switching on the A/C.","Use recirculation once the cabin starts to cool, and fresh air when the windows mist.","Park in the shade or use a sunshade, and keep the condenser clean from dust.","Have the system checked each year, and replace the cabin filter on time."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Diagnostic examples"},
      {"t":"p","text":"**Example 1: Cold air only on the driver's side.** A dual-zone car blows cool air on the right and hot air on the left. A scan tool shows the left blend door actuator reporting an error. The door is jammed by a piece of plastic. After removing it and relearning the door positions, both sides work."},
      {"t":"p","text":"**Example 2: Heater cold at idle.** A car heats at speed but not at idle. The coolant level is low, with an air lock in the heater. After bleeding and topping up, the heater is hot at idle. The cause was a leak from a hose clamp, which is repaired."},
      {"t":"p","text":"**Example 3: Musty smell and wet carpet.** A car smells of mould on the A/C and the passenger carpet is wet. The evaporator drain is blocked with leaves. Clearing it, replacing the cabin filter and treating the evaporator box stops the smell and the water."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"Always check the simple things first: cabin filter, fuse, blower speeds and the position of the controls. Many 'faults' are a blocked filter."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"A sweet smell and a damp carpet mean coolant. It is toxic and slippery, so find the source quickly."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m15-l11", "title": "Blower, resistor and actuator faults", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Many heating and air-conditioning complaints are electrical: no blower, blower on one speed only, no air on the left, or a clicking noise from behind the dashboard. This lesson covers the circuits and the tests that lead to quick repairs."},
      {"t":"h","text":"The blower motor circuit"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The blower motor is a permanent-magnet DC motor. At high speed it draws 15 to 25 A, depending on the vehicle. To make different speeds, there are two common methods. A **resistor pack** has resistors in series with the motor. The control selects low speeds through the largest resistances. The highest speed bypasses them and gives the motor full voltage. A **blower control module** uses transistors and varies the voltage by PWM, for smooth speed. Both parts sit in the airflow to be cooled."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Symptom","Likely cause"],"rows":[["Blower works on high only","A burnt resistor pack or module. The high speed bypasses them."],["Blower does not work at all","Fuse, relay, switch, motor, or a bad earth or connector"],["Blower works on some speeds","A resistor with an open circuit, or a worn switch contact"],["Blower noisy, rattles","Leaves or debris in the fan, or worn bearings"],["Blower runs by itself, will not stop","Stuck relay, module fault"],["Burning smell, blower fuse blows","A seized motor drawing too much current, or a short"],["Weak airflow, motor running","Blocked filter or intake, loose fan on the shaft, a stuck door"]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Testing a blower circuit"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Check that the fuse is intact and the relay clicks. Check the switch settings.","Measure the voltage at the motor connector with the blower on high. A good circuit shows near battery voltage (above about 11 V) on high.","If the voltage is good but the motor does not turn, the motor is faulty or has seized. Check the current and the fan for jamming.","If there is low voltage, find the voltage drop. Test the earth connection, the resistor pack and the connector. Look for heat damage and melted plastic.","Check the resistor pack. A burnt or open resistor shows an infinite reading. Replace the resistor pack and the connector together if the connector terminals are burnt.","Check the motor current with a clamp meter. A current higher than the specification points to a worn or tight bearing, a blocked fan or a shorted winding. A low current with a slow motor points to resistance in the circuit."]},
      {"t":"example","title":"Why a worn blower motor kills the resistor","steps":["A blower motor on high normally draws 15 A. A failing bearing makes the motor draw 22 A.","Power in the resistor pack on a medium speed is current squared times resistance. If the resistor is 0.5 Ω, the power rises from 15² × 0.5 = 112 W to 22² × 0.5 = 242 W, more than double.","The resistor overheats, burns open and fails. A new resistor fitted without checking the motor will burn out again. Always check the motor current when you replace a resistor."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Actuators and doors"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Doors that blend the air and direct it to the vents are driven by small motors. Some are simple DC motors with a position sensor, and some are stepper motors that count steps. A broken plastic gear gives a clicking noise as the motor turns but the door does not move. After a motor is replaced, or the battery disconnected, the doors need to be **calibrated** with a scan tool or by a procedure in the manual, so that the control module learns the end positions. Stuck doors, broken tabs and loose levers also cause faults. A mirror and a torch show whether the door moves when the control is changed."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Symptom","Likely cause"],"rows":[["Clicking from the dashboard when the temperature is changed","A stripped gear in a door actuator"],["Air from the wrong vents","A mode door stuck or a failed actuator or calibration"],["Temperature does not change","Blend door stuck, actuator failed, or a lost calibration"],["One side hot and the other cold on a dual-zone system","One zone's actuator or sensor"],["Fault codes for actuator position","Sensor wiring, motor or end stop damage"]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Checking and resetting"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Use the scan tool to run an **actuator test**, which moves each door in turn while you listen and look.","Look for **codes** for temperature sensors, door position and the blower.","After replacement, run the **calibration** and then check each setting and temperature at the vents.","Measure the vent temperatures on hot and cold, with the engine at working temperature.","Document the work, and the readings found."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"A burnt resistor or module is usually a symptom of a worn blower motor or a poor connection. Fix the cause or the part will fail again."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"Check the cabin filter and the blower wheel for debris when a blower is noisy. A single leaf can make a loud noise and cause a lot of worry."}
    ]}
  ],
  "practical": {"title":"Read A/C gauge pressures and diagnose from the readings","objective":"Under supervision, connect a manifold gauge set to a training or demonstration A/C system, record static and operating pressures at different engine speeds, measure vent temperature and diagnose from the readings.","time":"75 minutes","tools":["Manifold gauge set suitable for the refrigerant, with correct couplers","Thermometer for the vent temperature and an ambient thermometer","A training or demonstration A/C system or vehicle with the manual","Safety goggles and gloves","Certified recovery equipment (for any opening of the system) and a results table"],"safety":["Work only under the supervision of a certified technician or instructor.","Wear safety goggles and gloves at all times, because liquid refrigerant can freeze skin and eyes.","Never vent refrigerant to the air. Use certified recovery equipment for any opening of the system.","Keep hands and clothing clear of belts, fans and the clutch when the engine runs.","Make sure the gauge valves are closed before connecting the couplers."],"steps":["Read the label to find the refrigerant type, the charge weight and the oil type.","Check that the gauge set is for the same refrigerant, and that both valves are closed.","With the engine off, connect the blue hose to the low-side port and the red hose to the high-side port.","Read and record the static pressure on both gauges, and the ambient temperature.","Start the engine, set the A/C to maximum with the blower on high, and hold the speed at about 1,500 rpm.","Record the low-side and high-side pressures and the centre-vent temperature once they have settled.","Raise the speed to about 2,500 rpm and record the pressures again.","Compare each pressure with the chart for the ambient temperature.","Write down what the readings suggest, using the table of typical faults.","Switch off the engine and A/C, and wait for the pressures to equalise.","Disconnect the couplers carefully, replace the caps and close the gauge valves.","Complete your diagnosis note and clean the work area."],"checks":["I wore goggles and gloves and worked under supervision.","I connected the gauges to the correct ports and recorded static and operating pressures.","I compared readings with the chart for the ambient temperature.","I wrote a diagnosis from the readings and did not vent any refrigerant.","I disconnected the gauges safely and replaced the caps."]},
  "quiz": [
    {"id":"m15-q01","q":"Who may normally service an A/C system and handle refrigerant in many countries?","options":["A certified technician with the right equipment","The vehicle owner, using a hose and gauge from a local shop","Anyone who has bought a can of refrigerant from a parts shop","Any mechanic who has changed a battery and a set of tyres"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m15-l1","explain":"The law varies by country, but certification is often required. Check your local rules."},
    {"id":"m15-q02","q":"What must be done with refrigerant when a system has to be opened?","options":["It must be poured into a floor drain with plenty of water","It must be vented slowly to the open air outside the workshop doors","It must be burned in a safe place away from the workshop","It must be recovered with certified recovery equipment"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m15-l1","explain":"Venting is illegal and harms the environment."},
    {"id":"m15-q03","q":"What is the hazard of liquid refrigerant on skin or eyes?","options":["Acid burns only","Frostbite and eye damage","A rash that disappears after an hour","Electric shock"],"answer":1,"level":3,"lesson":"m15-l1","explain":"Refrigerant boils at very low temperatures, so it freezes tissue on contact."},
    {"id":"m15-q04","q":"Why should refrigerant gas not be allowed to collect in a pit?","options":["It is heavier than air and can cause suffocation","It sets off the fire alarm in the workshop each time","It makes the floor of the pit very slippery and unsafe to walk on","It rusts the tools that are left in the pit overnight"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m15-l1","explain":"Refrigerant gas is heavier than air, so it can collect in a pit or low area and push out the oxygen; always work with good ventilation."},
    {"id":"m15-q05","q":"Why should compressed air never be used to pressure test an A/C system?","options":["It can mix with the oil and explode","It would give a false pressure reading","It would make the pipes too cold","It would damage the gauge hoses"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m15-l1","explain":"Use only the nitrogen or the method that the maker approves."},
    {"id":"m15-q06","q":"Why must a recovery machine be calibrated and serviced?","options":["It makes the machine look newer","A wrong scale causes a wrong charge","It stops the machine from cooling the pipes","It is needed for the paint on the case"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m15-l1","explain":"The machine's accuracy depends on the scale, filters and seals. A small error in weight changes the performance of the system."},
    {"id":"m15-q07","q":"What colour is the hose that connects to the low-side port on a manifold gauge set?","options":["Red","Green","Blue","Yellow"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m15-l2","explain":"Red is for the high side and yellow is the service hose in the centre."},
    {"id":"m15-q08","q":"What does the yellow hose of a gauge set connect to?","options":["The low-side service port on the compressor suction line","The recovery machine, the vacuum pump or the refrigerant supply","The cabin air vent, to measure the outlet temperature","The high-side service port on the condenser outlet line"],"answer":1,"level":3,"lesson":"m15-l2","explain":"The yellow hose is the central service hose; it connects to the recovery machine, the vacuum pump or the refrigerant supply depending on the job."},
    {"id":"m15-q09","q":"The engine is off and the system has rested. What should the pressures on the two gauges be?","options":["The low side should be higher than the high side by a wide margin","About equal on both sides, and depending on the temperature","Both should read zero when the system is completely full","The high side should be much higher than the low side at once"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m15-l2","explain":"Static pressure rises as the temperature rises."},
    {"id":"m15-q10","q":"Both gauges read low and cooling is weak. What is the most likely cause?","options":["An overcharged system with far too much refrigerant in it","A low refrigerant charge from a leak","A blocked cabin filter that is cutting the airflow to the vents","A blocked condenser that cannot reject any heat to the air"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m15-l2","explain":"There is not enough refrigerant to generate the pressures."},
    {"id":"m15-q11","q":"Both gauges read high with an overheated condenser. What could be the cause?","options":["A blocked condenser, a failed condenser fan or an overcharge","A restriction in the low-side line before the evaporator inlet","A low refrigerant charge, because of a slow leak in a pipe joint","A weak compressor that is not building enough pressure to pump"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m15-l2","explain":"The condenser can not reject heat, so the pressure rises."},
    {"id":"m15-q12","q":"The low side reads high and the high side reads low. What does this suggest?","options":["A system that is overcharged with far too much refrigerant in it","A blocked cabin filter that is cutting the airflow to the vents","A weak compressor that is not pumping properly","A blocked expansion device that is starving the evaporator of refrigerant"],"answer":2,"level":1,"lesson":"m15-l2","explain":"The compressor can not build a pressure difference."},
    {"id":"m15-q13","q":"The low side reads very low or in vacuum and the high side is high. What does this suggest?","options":["A restriction, such as a blocked orifice tube or expansion valve","A condenser fan that runs too fast and over-cools the refrigerant","A low refrigerant charge, so that the compressor has little to pump","A faulty blower motor that is moving only a little air across it"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m15-l2","explain":"Refrigerant is held up before the restriction and the evaporator is starved."},
    {"id":"m15-q14","q":"Which method finds a leak that is in a hidden place, such as the evaporator?","options":["A careful look at the paintwork only","Pressing the brake pedal several times","UV dye, or a detector at the vent","A tyre pressure check on all wheels"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m15-l3","explain":"A hidden leak can show as dye in the drain or at the vent. A leak detector sniffed at the vent can also find it."},
    {"id":"m15-q15","q":"Why should you never connect a nitrogen cylinder directly to an A/C system?","options":["Cylinder pressure is far too high","Nitrogen is poisonous to breathe in","Nitrogen freezes the compressor oil","It is only allowed on certain days"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m15-l3","explain":"A nitrogen cylinder holds around 200 bar. Without a pressure regulator and a relief valve, the system could burst."},
    {"id":"m15-q16","q":"What can happen when sealant from a can is used in an A/C system?","options":["It always cures the leak permanently","It makes the oil thinner","It cools the condenser","It can block the valve and drier"],"answer":3,"level":3,"lesson":"m15-l3","explain":"Sealants are not a repair. They can clog small passages and contaminate the machines that service the system."},
    {"id":"m15-q17","q":"Why should a leak detector be moved below the line being checked?","options":["The gas is heavier than air","Refrigerant is lighter than air and rises","The detector works only upside down","The gas collects in the dust"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m15-l3","explain":"R134a and R1234yf are heavier than air, so a leak is more likely to be picked up below the pipe."},
    {"id":"m15-q18","q":"What is the correct order of the steps in an A/C repair?","options":["Repair, charge, recover, evacuate, with a leak check at the very end","Evacuate, recover, charge, repair, with a leak check as the first step","Recover, repair, evacuate and check for leaks, then charge","Charge, evacuate, repair, recover, with a leak check at the very end"],"answer":2,"level":3,"lesson":"m15-l4","explain":"The system must be empty to repair, and dry and tight before it is charged."},
    {"id":"m15-q19","q":"Why is the system evacuated with a vacuum pump?","options":["To add oil to the compressor","To remove air and boil off moisture","To cool the refrigerant before charging","To clean the condenser"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m15-l4","explain":"Moisture causes acid and ice, and air causes high pressures."},
    {"id":"m15-q20","q":"The vacuum falls on the gauge after the pump is switched off. What does this show?","options":["The vacuum pump is too powerful for the system and has stopped working","The refrigerant has been absorbed by the oil inside the compressor","The system is ready to charge, since the vacuum pump did its job","The system has a leak that must be found"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m15-l4","explain":"A system that holds vacuum has no large leak."},
    {"id":"m15-q21","q":"Why should a system be charged by weight rather than by pressure alone?","options":["The label gives only the pressure and not the weight of refrigerant","Pressure depends on temperature, but the correct weight does not change","Weight is easier to read on a gauge than pressure at the service port","Pressure never changes with temperature, so it cannot be used at all"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m15-l4","explain":"Use a scale or a charging station and the weight on the label."},
    {"id":"m15-q22","q":"Which is NOT a normal way of finding an A/C leak?","options":["Adding UV dye to the system and using a UV lamp afterwards","Using soapy water on accessible joints and watching for bubbles","Using an electronic leak detector along the pipes and fittings","Listening for a hiss from the tailpipe"],"answer":3,"level":3,"lesson":"m15-l4","explain":"The tailpipe is part of the exhaust system, not the A/C."},
    {"id":"m15-q23","q":"Why is a deep vacuum needed when evacuating a system?","options":["It makes the refrigerant lighter","It cools the compressor","It checks that the oil level is right","It lets moisture boil off"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m15-l5","explain":"At very low pressure, water boils at room temperature and is drawn out. A short, shallow vacuum leaves moisture behind."},
    {"id":"m15-q24","q":"During a vacuum hold test, the reading rises steadily to 10 kPa and keeps rising. What does this show?","options":["A successful evacuation","A blocked pump","A leak in the system","That the system is full of refrigerant"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m15-l5","explain":"A steady, continuing rise shows that air is entering. A small rise that levels off may only be moisture boiling."},
    {"id":"m15-q25","q":"A system needs 550 g, and 650 g is charged. What is the likely effect?","options":["Colder air at all of the vents","High pressure and poorer cooling","No change at all in performance","A lower compressor load than before"],"answer":1,"level":3,"lesson":"m15-l5","explain":"Too much charge raises the high-side pressure, loads the compressor, and the condenser cannot reject enough heat."},
    {"id":"m15-q26","q":"Should liquid refrigerant be charged into the low side of a running compressor?","options":["Yes, because it speeds up the charge","Yes, if the engine is off","Only when the oil is hot","No, it can damage the compressor"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m15-l5","explain":"A compressor cannot compress liquid. Liquid on the low side of a running system can break its valves and parts."},
    {"id":"m15-q27","q":"The clutch will not engage. Which should you check first?","options":["The tyre pressure on the rear wheels and the spare wheel","The brake fluid level in the reservoir on the bulkhead","The fuse, the relay and the power at the clutch coil","The cabin air filter only, because it controls the clutch"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m15-l6","explain":"Work through the electrical circuit from the fuse to the coil."},
    {"id":"m15-q28","q":"A compressor clutch works but the cooling is weak and the condenser is covered in dirt. What should you do?","options":["Clean the condenser, then check the pressures and the fan","Replace the evaporator, because it must have frozen up by now","Add more refrigerant straight away to raise the pressures again","Replace the compressor, because the dirt has damaged it already"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m15-l6","explain":"A dirty condenser raises the pressure and cuts the cooling."},
    {"id":"m15-q29","q":"Cooling stops after a while and then returns. What is a possible cause?","options":["A flat spare tyre in the boot of the vehicle, over time","A weak horn that sounds only when the engine is running","Moisture freezing at the expansion device, or a frozen evaporator","A burned-out interior bulb above the rear-view mirror in the cabin"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m15-l6","explain":"Freezing blocks the flow or the air until it melts."},
    {"id":"m15-q30","q":"What should you do if a system is low on refrigerant?","options":["Add some refrigerant every spring, before the hot weather starts","Add sealant to the system without finding where the leak is","Replace the compressor at once, because the leak is its fault","Find the leak, repair it and recharge by weight"],"answer":3,"level":1,"lesson":"m15-l6","explain":"A system that has lost refrigerant has a leak."},
    {"id":"m15-q31","q":"A pipe has a sharp change in temperature at a drier. What does this suggest?","options":["A fully charged system with no faults","A restriction at that point","A worn drive belt on the engine","A weak battery under the bonnet"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m15-l6","explain":"A blockage makes a pressure drop, which cools the refrigerant locally and often shows as frost on the outside."},
    {"id":"m15-q32","q":"Low gauge reads 1.0 bar and high gauge reads 8 bar at an ambient temperature of 35 °C. What is likely?","options":["An overcharge of the refrigerant","A low charge from a leak","A blocked condenser at the front","A weak battery under the bonnet"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m15-l7","explain":"Both pressures are low for a hot day. The system has lost refrigerant, so find the leak."},
    {"id":"m15-q33","q":"Gauges read 0.2 bar low and 14 bar high, with a frosted pipe after the drier. What does this suggest?","options":["A low charge of the refrigerant","An overcharge of the refrigerant","A restriction at the drier","A faulty condenser fan or relay"],"answer":2,"level":3,"lesson":"m15-l7","explain":"A very low low-side with a normal or high high-side points to a restriction. The frost marks the place where the pressure drops."},
    {"id":"m15-q34","q":"Gauges read 3.4 bar low and 26 bar high at idle. What should you check first?","options":["The condenser fan and airflow","The oil in the compressor sump","The windscreen washer reservoir","The tyre pressures on all wheels"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m15-l7","explain":"Both sides high, especially the high side, point to too little air over the condenser, or an overcharge, or air in the system."},
    {"id":"m15-q35","q":"At which set of conditions should gauge readings be taken?","options":["Engine off, with the blower switched off","About 1,500 rpm, A/C max, blower high","Engine at full throttle on the road","Engine idling with the A/C turned off"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m15-l7","explain":"Standard test conditions let you compare readings with the chart. Use a fan if the ambient temperature is high."},
    {"id":"m15-q36","q":"What is the purpose of the heater core?","options":["To heat the compressor oil so that it flows at start-up","To store hot water for the windscreen washers in winter","To warm the cabin air using hot engine coolant","To cool the engine coolant before it returns to the radiator"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m15-l8","explain":"It is a small radiator inside the HVAC box."},
    {"id":"m15-q37","q":"The blower works only on the highest speed. What is the likely cause?","options":["A weak compressor that is not building enough pressure","A burned-out blower resistor pack","A blocked condensate drain that is letting the water out slowly","A leaking heater core that is dripping coolant onto the carpet"],"answer":1,"level":3,"lesson":"m15-l8","explain":"The resistor steps for the lower speeds have failed."},
    {"id":"m15-q38","q":"What does the blend door do?","options":["It mixes hot and cold air to set the temperature","It switches between fresh outside air and recirculated cabin air","It filters the dust and the pollen out of the air from outside","It chooses between the face vents and the footwell outlets"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m15-l8","explain":"The mode door chooses the outlets and the recirculation door chooses the air source."},
    {"id":"m15-q39","q":"What is the best way to clear a misted windscreen?","options":["Open the bonnet, so that the moisture can escape from the cabin","Switch off the blower, so the glass can warm up on its own","Use fresh air, switch on the A/C, add heat and direct the air to the windscreen","Use recirculation with the heater off and the blower on low"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m15-l8","explain":"The A/C dries the air, and heat stops it from fogging again."},
    {"id":"m15-q40","q":"Both heater hoses feel the same temperature, hot. What does this suggest?","options":["Coolant is flowing through the core","The heater core is completely blocked","The thermostat is stuck closed shut","The compressor clutch has failed now"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m15-l8","explain":"Equal hot hoses show coolant circulation. If one is hot and the other cold, the core is blocked or there is an air lock."},
    {"id":"m15-q41","q":"The cabin air is 22 °C at 70% humidity. The dew point is about 16 °C. When will a windscreen at 8 °C mist?","options":["It will not mist, because the glass is cold","Only if the temperature rises to 30 °C","It will mist: glass is below dew point","Only when it is raining outside"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m15-l9","explain":"Moist air that touches a surface colder than its dew point condenses. The glass at 8 °C is below 16 °C, so it mists."},
    {"id":"m15-q42","q":"Why is the A/C often used for demisting the windscreen?","options":["It makes the glass much thicker","It raises the pressure at the vents","It cools the engine coolant down","The evaporator dries the air"],"answer":3,"level":1,"lesson":"m15-l9","explain":"Dry air has a lower dew point, so it can clear the glass without misting. The heater then warms it."},
    {"id":"m15-q43","q":"A sweet smell with misted windows and wet carpet suggests what?","options":["A blocked cabin filter","A dirty air filter","A leaking heater core","A worn drive belt"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m15-l9","explain":"Coolant has a sweet smell. A leaking core produces vapour that mists the glass, and liquid that wets the carpet."},
    {"id":"m15-q44","q":"Why is a driver's drowsiness with the heater on a reason to check the exhaust?","options":["The heater makes the driver sleepy","The engine oil is too thin","The tyres are over-inflated","CO may be entering the cabin"],"answer":3,"level":3,"lesson":"m15-l9","explain":"CO has no smell, but it causes headache and drowsiness. An exhaust leak near the cabin can allow it in."},
    {"id":"m15-q45","q":"What does a ticking noise behind the dash when you change the temperature often indicate?","options":["A leaking heater core that is dripping coolant into the footwell","A stripped gear or jammed door in an actuator","A weak cabin filter that is cutting the flow of air to the vents","A flat battery that has too little charge to move the motors"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m15-l10","explain":"Check the actuator and calibrate it after a replacement."},
    {"id":"m15-q46","q":"What is needed after replacing a climate actuator on many vehicles?","options":["A calibration (relearn) using a scan tool","A wheel alignment, so that the system learns the road position","A battery replacement, because the old one has lost the memory","A new cabin filter, because the old one holds the old settings"],"answer":0,"level":3,"lesson":"m15-l10","explain":"The system needs to learn the end stops of the new actuator."},
    {"id":"m15-q47","q":"What are signs of a leaking heater core?","options":["A burning smell and blue smoke from the exhaust pipe","A loud knock from the engine that gets worse under load","A flat tyre that keeps losing pressure overnight in the garage","Wet carpet, a sweet smell, coolant loss and a greasy film on the windscreen"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m15-l10","explain":"Confirm with a cooling system pressure test."},
    {"id":"m15-q48","q":"A customer in a hot climate says the car takes a long time to cool. What advice helps?","options":["Switch off the A/C and use fresh air only for the whole trip","Keep all the windows closed for the first ten minutes of the drive","Turn the engine off while the A/C runs, so the cabin stays cool","Let the hot air out first, then use recirculation and park in the shade"],"answer":3,"level":1,"lesson":"m15-l10","explain":"Hot air trapped in the cabin takes a long time to cool."},
    {"id":"m15-q49","q":"Why might water collect in the passenger footwell on a hot day?","options":["A weak battery that cannot run the blower motor at all","A failed horn relay that is draining the battery overnight","A leaking fuel tank under the rear seat of the vehicle","A blocked condensate drain from the evaporator box"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m15-l10","explain":"The condensate has nowhere to go and overflows into the cabin."},
    {"id":"m15-q50","q":"Cold air comes from the driver's side and hot air from the passenger's side of a dual-zone system. What is likely?","options":["A low refrigerant charge","A weak compressor","A blend door or actuator","A blocked radiator"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m15-l10","explain":"Each zone has its own blend door. A fault in one actuator or its calibration affects just that side."},
    {"id":"m15-q51","q":"The blower works only on its highest speed. What is the likely fault?","options":["A burnt resistor pack or module","A faulty wheel bearing on the car","A weak battery in the vehicle","A stuck brake caliper on a wheel"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m15-l11","explain":"The high speed bypasses the resistors, so it still works. The lower speeds go through the resistors, which have burnt open."},
    {"id":"m15-q52","q":"Why should you check the blower motor current when replacing a resistor?","options":["A resistor does not need any check","A worn motor will burn the new resistor","The motor current has no effect on the resistor","Resistors last forever if the motor current is low"],"answer":1,"level":3,"lesson":"m15-l11","explain":"A motor drawing too much current raises the heat in the resistor more than proportionally, so the new part would fail."},
    {"id":"m15-q53","q":"A clicking noise behind the dashboard when the temperature is changed suggests what?","options":["A low refrigerant charge in the system","A blocked cabin filter in the box","A weak compressor on the engine","A stripped gear in an actuator"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m15-l11","explain":"The actuator motor turns but the broken gear cannot move the door, which causes the click."},
    {"id":"m15-q54","q":"What must be done after replacing a climate actuator or disconnecting the battery?","options":["Calibrate the doors","Replace the cabin filter","Add refrigerant","Change the compressor oil"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m15-l11","explain":"The module has to relearn the end positions of the doors, or the temperature and air distribution will be wrong."}
  ],
  "cards": [
    {"id":"m15-c01","front":"Who may service an A/C system?","back":"A certified technician with approved equipment, in many countries.","lesson":"m15-l1"},
    {"id":"m15-c02","front":"What must you never do with refrigerant?","back":"Vent it to the air. Recover it with certified equipment.","lesson":"m15-l1"},
    {"id":"m15-c03","front":"What are the hazards of refrigerant?","back":"Frostbite, eye damage, suffocation in low areas and fire for flammable types.","lesson":"m15-l1"},
    {"id":"m15-c04","front":"What colour is the low-side hose?","back":"Blue.","lesson":"m15-l2"},
    {"id":"m15-c05","front":"What colour is the high-side hose?","back":"Red.","lesson":"m15-l2"},
    {"id":"m15-c06","front":"What is the yellow hose for?","back":"The service hose to recovery, vacuum or the supply.","lesson":"m15-l2"},
    {"id":"m15-c07","front":"What is static pressure?","back":"The equal pressure on both sides with the engine off, depending on temperature.","lesson":"m15-l2"},
    {"id":"m15-c08","front":"What do both gauges low suggest?","back":"Low refrigerant charge.","lesson":"m15-l2"},
    {"id":"m15-c09","front":"What do both gauges high suggest?","back":"Overcharge, a poorly cooled condenser or air in the system.","lesson":"m15-l2"},
    {"id":"m15-c10","front":"What does low-side high and high-side low suggest?","back":"A weak compressor.","lesson":"m15-l2"},
    {"id":"m15-c11","front":"What does very low low-side and high high-side suggest?","back":"A restriction such as a blocked orifice tube.","lesson":"m15-l2"},
    {"id":"m15-c12","front":"Name three ways to find a refrigerant leak.","back":"Electronic detector, UV dye and lamp, bubble solution, nitrogen pressure test and vacuum hold test.","lesson":"m15-l3"},
    {"id":"m15-c13","front":"Why are stop-leak products not a repair?","back":"They can block small passages and contaminate equipment, and they do not fix the cause.","lesson":"m15-l3"},
    {"id":"m15-c14","front":"Maximum nitrogen test pressure?","back":"Use the manual's value, typically no more than about 10 bar, with a regulator and relief valve.","lesson":"m15-l3"},
    {"id":"m15-c15","front":"What is the order of A/C repair?","back":"Recover, repair, evacuate and leak check, then charge by weight.","lesson":"m15-l4"},
    {"id":"m15-c16","front":"Why evacuate?","back":"To remove air and boil off moisture.","lesson":"m15-l4"},
    {"id":"m15-c17","front":"Why charge by weight?","back":"Pressure depends on temperature; the correct weight does not.","lesson":"m15-l4"},
    {"id":"m15-c18","front":"How do you find a leak?","back":"UV dye, an electronic detector or soapy water.","lesson":"m15-l4"},
    {"id":"m15-c19","front":"Why evacuate for a long time?","back":"To lower the pressure until moisture boils away. A deep vacuum is about 0.1 kPa or about 750 microns.","lesson":"m15-l5"},
    {"id":"m15-c20","front":"What does a vacuum hold test show?","back":"A steady rise in pressure shows a leak. A small rise that levels off may be moisture.","lesson":"m15-l5"},
    {"id":"m15-c21","front":"How do you charge accurately?","back":"By weight on a calibrated scale, to the amount on the label, never by feel or gauge pressure alone.","lesson":"m15-l5"},
    {"id":"m15-c22","front":"What do you check first if the clutch will not engage?","back":"The fuse, relay and power at the coil.","lesson":"m15-l6"},
    {"id":"m15-c23","front":"What does a dirty condenser cause?","back":"High pressure and weak cooling.","lesson":"m15-l6"},
    {"id":"m15-c24","front":"Low and low gauge readings?","back":"A low charge from a leak.","lesson":"m15-l7"},
    {"id":"m15-c25","front":"Very low low-side, high or normal high-side?","back":"A restriction in the drier, orifice tube or expansion valve.","lesson":"m15-l7"},
    {"id":"m15-c26","front":"Both sides high, with high side very high?","back":"Overcharge, poor condenser airflow or air in the system.","lesson":"m15-l7"},
    {"id":"m15-c27","front":"Low side high, high side low?","back":"A weak compressor.","lesson":"m15-l7"},
    {"id":"m15-c28","front":"What conditions are used for gauge tests?","back":"About 1,500 rpm, A/C max, blower high, doors open, and a note of the ambient temperature.","lesson":"m15-l7"},
    {"id":"m15-c29","front":"What does a blower working only on high suggest?","back":"A burned-out resistor pack.","lesson":"m15-l8"},
    {"id":"m15-c30","front":"What is the heater core?","back":"A small radiator that warms the air using engine coolant.","lesson":"m15-l8"},
    {"id":"m15-c31","front":"What does the blend door do?","back":"It mixes hot and cold air to set the temperature.","lesson":"m15-l8"},
    {"id":"m15-c32","front":"How do you demist a windscreen?","back":"Fresh air, A/C on, heat on and air to the windscreen.","lesson":"m15-l8"},
    {"id":"m15-c33","front":"What is the dew point?","back":"The temperature at which water vapour in the air condenses. Glass colder than this mists.","lesson":"m15-l9"},
    {"id":"m15-c34","front":"How do you clear a misted windscreen?","back":"Use fresh air, A/C on to dry the air, and the heater to warm it. Do not use recirculation.","lesson":"m15-l9"},
    {"id":"m15-c35","front":"What does a sweet smell with wet carpet mean?","back":"A leaking heater core.","lesson":"m15-l9"},
    {"id":"m15-c36","front":"Types of cabin filter?","back":"Particle, activated carbon, and combination or anti-allergen.","lesson":"m15-l9"},
    {"id":"m15-c37","front":"What does a ticking behind the dash suggest?","back":"A stripped actuator gear or a jammed door.","lesson":"m15-l10"},
    {"id":"m15-c38","front":"What may be needed after replacing an actuator?","back":"A calibration with a scan tool.","lesson":"m15-l10"},
    {"id":"m15-c39","front":"What are signs of a leaking heater core?","back":"Wet carpet, a sweet smell, coolant loss and a greasy windscreen film.","lesson":"m15-l10"},
    {"id":"m15-c40","front":"What blocked part causes water in the footwell?","back":"The condensate drain.","lesson":"m15-l10"},
    {"id":"m15-c41","front":"What helps a hot car cool faster?","back":"Ventilate first, then use recirculation, and park in the shade.","lesson":"m15-l10"},
    {"id":"m15-c42","front":"Blower works only on high. Cause?","back":"A burnt resistor pack or blower module.","lesson":"m15-l11"},
    {"id":"m15-c43","front":"Why does a worn blower motor burn the resistor?","back":"Excess current makes the resistor heat up more than in proportion, so it burns open.","lesson":"m15-l11"},
    {"id":"m15-c44","front":"What is needed after an actuator replacement?","back":"Calibration of the doors with a scan tool.","lesson":"m15-l11"}
  ]
});
